.venv-hpc/
__pycache__/
.pytest_cache/
data/raw/
data/processed/
# `runs/*` and not `runs/`: git cannot re-include a file whose parent directory is excluded,
# and excluding the directory made every negation below a statement of intent that only
# `git add -f` could carry out. Excluding the entries instead leaves the directory itself
# visible, so a negation for a file directly inside it works as written. Subdirectories are
# still excluded as directories, which is what we want -- nothing re-includes through one.
runs/*
*.egg-info/
.env

# The authoritative cluster results for the GB1 sweep, recovered from Slurm stdout.
# runs/ is otherwise untracked, but this is the only record of the figures the Week 4
# debrief quotes, and local re-runs do not reproduce all of them (see ARCHITECTURE.md).
!runs/binding_cluster_finals.csv

# The agent-policy comparison on the GB1 pilot: the arm Slurm job 19202 never ran.
# runs/ is otherwise untracked, but this is the only record that the LLM policy was
# measured against the heuristics, and the conclusion is a negative worth keeping.
!runs/agent_policy_comparison.csv
runs/twins/
runs/orbit/
runs/monomers/
runs/monitor/
runs/improve/

# The coverage-channel experiment: whether equalising axis coverage removes the advantage a
# policy gets from counting offers. runs/ is otherwise untracked, but this is the only record
# that the obvious repair was tried and does not work -- it relocates the advantage into
# dossier order rather than closing it -- and a repair that looks right and is not is exactly
# the thing a later reader would otherwise redo.
#
# Note on the negations in this file: they used to be a statement of intent rather than the
# mechanism. `runs/` excluded the directory, so git could not re-include anything inside it,
# and every one of these files is tracked only because it was added with `git add -f`. The
# top of the file now excludes `runs/*` instead, so a negation for a file directly inside
# `runs/` does what it says and a new matching file is picked up without a force-add.
!runs/coverage_channel.json

# Genome-to-ranked-targets, validated on three virus families with the pathogen held out of
# the reference each time. runs/ is otherwise untracked, but this is the record that the
# ranking recovers the targets with drugs against them across coronavirus, paramyxovirus and
# orthomyxovirus rather than on the one family it was built against.
!runs/intake_validation.json

# The holdout ladder: how distant the nearest curated relative may be before identification
# stops. runs/ is otherwise untracked, but this is the record that the validation above is an
# isolate holdout rather than a species one, and that one rung further out the pipeline
# returns nothing. A limit nobody measured would be assumed to be further away than it is.
!runs/holdout_ladder.json

# The three-tier identification cascade: what is recoverable past the cliff the ladder found,
# and by which mechanism. runs/ is otherwise untracked, but this is the record that conserved
# enzymes survive cross-family homology while surface antigens do not and must be recovered
# from their own sequence instead.
!runs/identification_cascade.json


# Drug-target ranks against pathogen genome input, one row per target, with the denominator
# and the rank-sum probability on every row. runs/ is otherwise untracked, but this is the
# deliverable the collaborator reads and the record that the ranking beats chance on three of
# four pathogens and not on Nipah.
!runs/target_ranks.csv

# Surface markers scored the way a macrocycle sees a target, with the contact-cutoff
# sensitivity beside them. runs/ is otherwise untracked, but this is the record that scoring
# a contiguous hydrophobic patch ranks a flat interface above a deep enzyme pocket, which is
# the reverse of a small-molecule druggability score and the right way round here.
!runs/surface_markers.json

# The attempt to score on those markers, and why it was withdrawn. runs/ is otherwise
# untracked, but a change that was measured, made the ranking worse in two different forms and
# was reverted is exactly the record a later reader needs in order not to retry it.
!runs/surface_in_ranker.json

# Scoping the 2.0-point localisation term to the chain being scored, and the one family it
# makes worse. runs/ is otherwise untracked, but this is the record of a change that trips the
# rejection criterion written for it and was kept anyway, which is the kind of decision that
# has to be auditable or it reads as nobody having checked. It also carries the measurement
# that an earlier automated estimate of the blast radius was wrong by a factor of seventeen.
!runs/surface_scoped_per_chain.json

# Why the remote homology tier published a fish nidovirus as two pneumoviruses' top targets,
# and what it took to stop it. runs/ is otherwise untracked, but this record carries three
# things nothing else does: that no score separates a genuine cross-family hit from a
# compositional artifact, that the quantity which appeared to is a length filter in disguise
# and was refused, and that the correct same-genus answer was in the reference at seed rank
# 962 the whole time -- so the tier's real defect is its prefilter and is still open.
!runs/remote_span_licensing.json

# The macrocycle-amenability gate and why its validation is inconclusive. runs/ is otherwise
# untracked, but a result recorded as inconclusive, with the defects that made it so, is
# worth more than the apparent agreement it would otherwise have reported. Regenerated by
# `python -m hpc_pilots gate` over every target configs/structures.json declares, so a target
# missing from the gate is now missing from the config, where the absence is visible.
!runs/amenability_gate.json

# One genome in, and what each stage of the onset arm handed the next. runs/ is otherwise
# untracked, but this is the record that no ranked candidate is dropped between stages -- the
# surface stage emits one row per chain it was handed, measured or refused with the reason --
# and it is what the notebook's flowchart, its stage ledger and its score decomposition are
# all drawn from. Written by `python -m hpc_pilots chain`.
!runs/onset_chain.json

# The twelve ORFs stage 1 ranked on the held-out SARS-CoV-2 genome, each with its score split
# into the five stated weights. runs/ is otherwise untracked, but the chain record above keeps
# this stage as five counts, so without this one the published page can say that nine of
# twelve ORFs were identified and cannot say which nine, what they scored, or what the other
# three were. It is the landing page's Part 1 table. Separate from the chain record because it
# is the cheap end of the pipeline -- about seventeen seconds against a warm reference cache,
# where the whole chain is thirty-five minutes -- so the table can be refreshed without
# re-running six stages to do it. Written by `python -m hpc_pilots candidates`.
!runs/intake_candidates.json

# The same five stages on three pathogens from three viral families, each held out from its
# own reference. runs/ is otherwise untracked, but this is the record that the no-dropped-rows
# invariant holds on every genome while the coverage behind it does not -- influenza keeps a
# measurable surface on none of its ranked chains, because its reviewed entries are split so
# finely by strain that the nearest relative of a held-out protein has no structure of its
# own. Written by `python -m hpc_pilots chain --genomes`.
!runs/onset_chain_genomes.json

# Every chain record, including the ones strangers ask for through the site. One genome's run
# is the smallest complete unit of evidence this system produces -- five stages, every score
# decomposed, one surface row per ranked chain -- and the page published for it is generated
# from the record rather than the other way round. Tracking them here means the record is the
# committed thing and `docs/results/` holds a copy, rather than a web output directory being
# the only home of a scientific result. Written by
# `python -m hpc_pilots chain --accession <id> --pathogen <name>`, and by the run-chain
# workflow when a request comes in through the site.
!runs/onset_chain_*.json

# What the landing page's `structures.predicted` scalars are actually counting. runs/ is
# otherwise untracked, but this is the record that the endpoint label "AlphaFold DB" names a
# host and not a provenance: of the 54 predicted models the held-out run's accessions carry,
# 35 are served by the AlphaFold host and none was computed by AlphaFold -- every one is
# ColabFold v1.5.2. The chain record cannot hold this, because `_evidence_summary` reduces
# any list to a count by design, so the distinction has to be recorded somewhere and the
# page's claim has to be checkable against it. Written by
# `python -m hpc_pilots.predicted_inventory`.
!runs/predicted_models.json

# The same inventory for a pathogen run through `chain.one`, one file per genome beside that
# genome's own chain record. Tracked for the same reason `runs/onset_chain_*.json` is: the
# record is the committed thing, and a page generated from it should be checkable against a
# file in the tree rather than against a run nobody kept.
!runs/predicted_models_*.json
.venv-pfn/

# The notebook points MPLCONFIGDIR here so a stale user-level matplotlibrc cannot print a
# backend warning into a demo. It is a cache, never a record.
notebooks/.mplcache/
.mplcache/
notebooks/.ipynb_checkpoints/
